{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Command Aaron.</b> Rashi explained: צו is nothing other than an urging — immediately and for generations. Urging: as it is written in the portion of Devarim, ״ואצו את שופטיכם.״ And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: it shall be an urging like a rod and a strap. Immediately: as it is written in the portion of Naso, ״צו את בני ישראל וישלחו מן המחנה״ etc., and there it is written, ״ויעשו כן בני ישראל.״ For generations: as it is written in the portion of Shelach, ״מן היום אשר צוה ה׳ והלאה לדורותיכם.״ And because in the matter of arranging the fire and in the matter of removing the ashes, laziness can set in, the language of urging is written concerning them. Furthermore Rashi explained: Rabbi Shimon said, Scripture needs all the more to urge in a matter where there is financial loss — meaning, if he did not perform the matter properly, another must be done in its place, and the first is voided and lost, and behold that is financial loss." "<b>Aaron.</b> Therefore it is written, ״וזרקו בני אהרן״ and ״וערכו בני אהרן.״ Moses said before HaKadosh Baruch Hu: Master of the universe — a hated pit whose waters are hated, meaning, Aaron is hated before You on account of the matter of the calf. There it was taught: all woods are fit for the fire-pile except olive wood and vine wood [because the oil for the menorah and the wine for libations come from them]; for the woods, a portion of honor is assigned on account of the fruit, but for Aaron no honor is assigned on account of the sons? Immediately: ״צו את אהרן.״" "<b>This is the law of the burnt offering.</b> Until now we did not know from when is the time of the daily morning burnt offering and until when is the time of the daily evening burnt offering; therefore it says, ״זאת תורת העולה״ — the one singled out among burnt offerings, and which is it? The daily burnt offering. And so all the sacrifices stated in the portion of Vayikra, it returns and states here all their laws." "<b>It is the burnt offering.</b> Even though it is offered up during the day, as it is written, ״ביום הקריבו את זבחו״ — the verse teaches about it that its fat and its limbs are valid all night." "<b>And the fire of the altar.</b> The inner altar." "<b>Shall be kept burning on it.</b> Kindled from the fire of the outer altar, as stated in the Tosefta: from where do we know that the fire of the inner altar must be only from the outer altar? Scripture says: ואש המזבח תוקד בו." "<b>On it.</b> From it. We find bet in place of mem, as in ומשחת בו — ביום המשח אותו. The coal-pan and the menorah — from where? A fortiori: אש is said of the inner altar and אש is said of the coal-pan and the menorah; just as the fire of the inner altar is from the outer altar, so too the fire of the coal-pan and the menorah is from the outer altar."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {1 "The bracketed text appears in the source; it is a parenthetical explanation of why olive and vine woods are excluded."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:22.670087536Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}